on" tabindex="0"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button docs-ml-promotion-no-button">NO, THANKS</span></span></span><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-container"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button-wrapper docs-ml-promotion-yes-button-wrapper" role="button" tabindex="0"><span class="docs-ml-promotion-action-button docs-ml-promotion-yes-button">USE THE APP</span></span></span></div></div></div></div><div id="docs-ml-header-id" class="docs-ml-header"><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-drive-link"><div id="docs-ml-header-drive-link-aria-label" style="display:none;">Go to Drive</div><a href="https://drive.google.com/u/2?usp=docs_web" aria-labelledby="docs-ml-header-drive-link-aria-label" role="button"><div class="docs-ml-header-icon-container"><div class="docs-ml-icon docs-ml-header-icon docs-ml-arrow-back-dir"><div class="docs-ml-img-container docs-ml-img docs-ml-arrow-back-icon"></div></div></div></a></span><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-document-title"><div class="docs-ml-header-document-title-text">Hermes Skills Architectural Analysis</div></span><div id="docs-ml-header-edit-button-aria-label" style="display:none;">Edit</div><span class="docs-ml-header-item docs-ml-header-edit-button" aria-labelledby="docs-ml-header-edit-button-aria-label" role="button" tabindex="0"><div class="docs-ml-header-icon-container"><div class="docs-ml-icon docs-ml-header-icon"><div class="docs-ml-img-container docs-ml-img docs-ml-edit-icon"></div></div></div></span></div><div class="app-container"><div class="doc-container"><div class="doc"><div class="doc-content" style="background-color:#ffffff;max-width:468pt;padding:72pt 72pt 72pt 72pt"><h1 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:24pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:24pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes Skills Architecture and Procedural Extensibility Boundaries</span></h1><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A Hermes Skill is an on-demand procedural capability package structured as a standardized filesystem artifact compliant with the agentskills.io open specification. It integrates YAML frontmatter metadata, structured Markdown operational procedures, and optional supporting assets (deterministic helper scripts, output templates, and reference schemas). A skill is fundamentally non-executable in isolation; it contains no private execution runtime, background daemon, or autonomous transport logic. Instead, it functions as externalized procedural memory that is ingested into the large language model's (LLM) reasoning context through a three-level progressive disclosure protocol. Once loaded, the skill shapes the model's cognitive trajectory, instructing it on how to sequence native tools, execute command-line utilities via the host terminal, handle known edge-case failures, and verify output artifacts.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The native skills mechanism can extend Hermes across any deterministic or multi-step engineering workflow that can be decomposed into natural language operational instructions, shell command invocations, helper scripts (Python, Bash, Node.js), and native tool compositions. Skills excel at codifying domain methodologies (such as test-driven development, git pull request lifecycles, and bioinformatics data extraction), orchestrating multi-agent fan-out hierarchies via delegate_task, wrapping external command-line interfaces (CLIs), and establishing self-healing error recovery loops. Because skills support dynamic variable substitution, sandbox secret passthrough, and autonomous self-evolution via the native skill_manage tool and the background Curator maintenance engine, the agent can programmatically author, patch, and retire its own procedural catalog based on operational task feedback.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Procedural skills reach an architectural boundary when a system requirement demands active computational runtimes or integration hooks that exceed the agent's installed toolset. Skills cannot provide long-running background daemon listeners, manage bidirectional asynchronous event streams, enforce strict compile-time type validation, isolate sensitive credentials without host environment exposure, or perform continuous, compute-heavy media rendering and neural inference. Furthermore, skills cannot connect directly to external Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers; MCP integrations exist strictly at the Hermes Core tool gateway layer, where tools must be registered before any skill can instruct the agent to call them. Consequently, extending Hermes beyond procedural orchestration requires registering native Python tools, provisioning MCP servers, or decoupling compute-intensive operations into external asynchronous worker services.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Architectural System Mechanics</span></h2><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Structural Anatomy and On-Disk Layout</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">A Hermes Skill is packaged as a discrete folder within the agent's filesystem search hierarchy. Its core component is a mandatory SKILL.md document that pairs machine-readable YAML frontmatter with human- and model-readable operational instructions. The directory layout follows a modular progressive disclosure architecture:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_1-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">SKILL.md: Mandatory file containing YAML frontmatter bounded by --- dividers, followed by structured Markdown procedures, trigger definitions, and verification criteria.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">scripts/: Optional directory housing deterministic executable helper code (Python, Bash, Node.js). Offloading algorithmic parsing, data transformations, or complex API calls to scripts preserves LLM context tokens and avoids runtime code generation errors.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">references/: Optional directory storing static reference documentation, API specifications, database schemas, or lookup tables. These files are not loaded into context until explicitly requested via Level 2 progressive disclosure.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">templates/: Optional directory containing boilerplate file structures, configuration templates, or JSON schemas that the agent copies or populates during execution.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">assets/: Optional directory containing static media, read-only binary assets, or fixtures required by the workflow.</span></li></ul><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Official maintainer authoring standards enforce explicit constraints on this layout. The SKILL.md document is subject to a hard content limit of 100,000 characters (MAX_SKILL_CONTENT_CHARS), though production skills are recommended to target 8,000 to 14,000 characters (approximately 100 to 200 lines of Markdown). Frontmatter descriptions are strictly limited to 60 characters or fewer and must end with a period, ensuring that Level 0 discovery catalogs remain concise and do not degrade prompt caching efficiency.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Frontmatter Schema Specification</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The YAML frontmatter defines runtime prerequisites, toolset dependencies, environmental bindings, and scheduling directives. The complete official schema encompasses:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">---<br>name: production-deployment<br>description: Automates service deployment and health verification.<br>version: 1.1.0<br>author: Systems Infrastructure Team<br>license: Apache-2.0<br>platforms: [linux, macos]<br><br>metadata:<br> &nbsp;hermes:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;tags: [DevOps, Deployment, Kubernetes]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;related_skills: [docker-build, cluster-triage]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;# Tool and Toolset Conditional Activation<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;requires_toolsets: [terminal, file]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;requires_tools: [terminal]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;fallback_for_toolsets: [cloud_deploy]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;fallback_for_tools: [native_k8s_deploy]<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;# Non-secret Configuration (stored in ~/.hermes/config.yaml under skills.config)<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;config:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;- key: k8s.cluster_url<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;description: API endpoint for the target cluster<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;default: "https://k8s.internal.net:6443"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;prompt: "Cluster API endpoint URL"<br><br> &nbsp; &nbsp;# Blueprint Specification (Suggested Cron Automation)<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;blueprint:<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;schedule: "0 2 * * *"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;deliver: origin<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;prompt: "Execute scheduled cluster deployment verification and prune dead replica sets."<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;no_agent: false<br><br># Secret Environment Variables (stored in ~/.hermes/.env)<br>required_environment_variables:<br> &nbsp;- name: KUBE_AUTH_TOKEN<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;prompt: "Enter Kubernetes cluster token"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;help: "Service account JWT token from cluster secrets"<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;required_for: "cluster authentication"<br><br># Disk-based credentials relative to ~/.hermes/ (mounted into sandboxes)<br>required_credential_files:<br> &nbsp;- path: kubernetes/kubeconfig.yaml<br> &nbsp; &nbsp;description: "Cluster configuration and certificates"<br>---<br></span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Markdown body following the frontmatter must implement five standardized, sequential sections:</span></p><ol class="lst-kix_list_2-0 start" start="1" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal"># Title: Formal name and high-level objective of the procedure.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">## When to Use: Precise activation criteria and negative boundaries defining when the agent must not load the skill.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">## Quick Reference: Compact syntax tables detailing CLI commands, parameters, or endpoints.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">## Procedure: Numbered, deterministic operational instructions.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">## P[span_176](start_span)[span_176](end_span)[span_186](start_span)[span_186](end_span)itfalls: Known operational failure modes, environment-specific bugs, and remediation paths.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">## V[span_177](start_span)[span_177](end_span)[span_187](start_span)[span_187](end_span)erification: Concrete, checkable criteria (command output, file hash, or artifact state) that confirm task completion.</span></li></ol><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Discovery Hierarchy and Conditional Gating</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes discovers skills by indexing multiple directory tiers upon engine initialization:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Primary Local Directory (~/.hermes/skills/): The root source of truth for user-created, agent-authored, and hub-installed packages.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Configured External Directories (skills.external_dirs): Arbitrary filesystem directories declared in [span_138](start_span)[span_138](end_span)[span_156](start_span)[span_156](end_span)~/.hermes/config.yaml, merged into the registry at runtime.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Project-Local Directories (&lt;project-root&gt;/.hermes/skills/ or &lt;project-root&gt;/.agents/skills/): Repository-specific procedures. To guard against repository-based prompt injection, project-local skills are disabled by default until an operator executes hermes skills trust within the directory.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Plugin Skills: Namespaced skills (plugin:skill) provided by installed plugins. These are omitted from global discovery listings to prevent index bloat and must be invoked explicitly via qualified calls.</span></li></ul><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Discovery precedence dictates that local definitions in ~/.hermes/skills/ shadow identically named skills residing in external directories or default repository distributions. Non-existent directories in configuration paths are skipped silently without throwing runtime exceptions.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Conditional activation logic filters discovered skills before registering them in the active session:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_4-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Platform Boundaries: The platforms field (e.g., platforms: [linux]) suppresses the skill from the discovery index, system prompt, and slash-command completion on non-matching host operating systems.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Prerequisite Toolsets (requires_tools, requires_toolsets): A skill is hidden from the agent if its required native tools (such as terminal or web_searc[span_140](start_span)[span_140](end_span)[span_158](start_span)[span_158](end_span)h) are disabled in the active session profile.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Fallback Tool Activation (fallback_for_tools, fallback_for_toolsets): A skill declares itself as an operational workaround. For example, a duckduckgo-sea[span_141](start_span)[span_141](end_span)[span_159](start_span)[span_159](end_span)rch skill with fallback_for_tools: [web_search] remains invisible when the primary API-backed search tool is operational, but activates automatically if the primary tool lacks credentials or is disabled.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The Progressive Disclosure Token Optimization Protocol</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">To prevent procedural documentation from overwhelming context windows or invalidating provider prompt caching, Hermes implements a three-level loading protocol:</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">Progressive Disclosure Context Traversal:<br><br>Level 0: System Prompt Discovery Index (~3,000 tokens)<br> &nbsp;| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Exposes: [{name, description, category}]<br> &nbsp;| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Condition: Always resident across session turns<br> &nbsp;v<br>Level 1: Procedural Context Ingestion (skill_view(name))<br> &nbsp;| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Exposes: Full SKILL.md body, substituted variables, runtime configs<br> &nbsp;| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;Condition: Triggered by user slash command or autonomous LLM intent match<br> &nbsp;v<br>Level 2: Reference File Traversal (skill_view(name, path))<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Exposes: Isolated documents in references/*.md or templates/<br> &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Condition: Triggered on-demand when deep documentation is queried<br></span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Level 0 discovery costs approximately 3,000 tokens across an entire library, establishing awareness without burdening baseline inference. Level 1 loads the full operational guide only when relevance is determined. Level 2 guarantees that voluminous technical specifications or extended lookup tables consume tokens only during the specific turn in which they are consulted.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Dynamic Variable and Secret Ingestion</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When Level 1 loading occurs via skill_view, Hermes evaluates the document and applies runtime substitutions before presenting the instructions to the model:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_5-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Directory Substitution (${HERMES_SKILL_DIR}): Evaluated to the absolute filesystem path of the loaded skill directory. Instructions use this variable to invoke bundled scripts without ambiguous path traversal (e.g., python3 ${HERMES_SKILL_DIR}/scripts/validate.py).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Session Correlation (${HERMES_SESSION_ID}): Evaluated to the active session UUID, allowing scripts to namespace temporary working files.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Non-Secret Configuration: Variables declared under metadata.hermes.config are resolved against skill[span_116](start_span)[span_116](end_span)[span_120](start_span)[span_120](end_span)s.config in ~/.her[span_194](start_span)[span_194](end_span)mes/config.yaml and appended to the context payload.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Secret Sandbox Passthrough: Variables declared under required_environmen[span_117](start_span)[span_117](end_span)[span_121](start_span)[span_121](end_span)t_variables are read from ~/.hermes/.env. Missing variables trigger interactive CLI prompts during local runs. When the skill runs, these secrets are exported directly into the process environment of execution sandboxes (terminal, execute_code, Docker), enabling scripts to authenticate against APIs without exposing plaintext keys in prompt tokens.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Credential File Mounting: Files defined in required_credential_files (e.g., OAuth tokens, certificates) are verified on disk and bind-mounted as read-only paths inside containerized sandboxes.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Host Shell Execution (!`cmd`): If skills.inline_shell: true is explicitly configured, inline shell snippets embedded in SKILL.md execute on the host shell during Level 1 loading. Their standard output is inlined into the message (capped at 4,000 characters per snippet) before the LLM parses the turn.</span></li></ul><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">### Autonomous Self-Evolution and the Curator Engine Hermes implements an autonomous procedural learning loop through the built-in skill_manage tool and the background Curator maintenance engine. When the agent completes an intricate multi-turn task (typically five or more tool interactions), resolves a critical runtime failure, or receives instructions via /learn, it invokes skill_manage to codify the workflow. The tool supports five core actions: create (writes a complete new SKILL.md under skills.create_dir or ~/.hermes/skills/), patch (applies search-and-replace edits using old_string and new_string), write_file (creates or overwrites supporting assets in scripts/ or references/), remove_file (deletes obsolete assets), and delete (unlinks the entire skill folder). If skills.write_approval: true is set, all agent-directed mutations are held in an approval queue awaiting user confirmation.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">To prevent procedural sprawl and catalog pollution, the Curator operates as a background daemon monitoring skill health. The Curator tracks usage metrics in an append-only ledger (~/.hermes/skills/.curator_ledger.jsonl) and individual metadata records (.usage.json), recording use_count, view_count, patch_count, and last activity timestamps. The Curator's authority is strictly bounded: it only manages skills marked with created_by: agent. Bundled skills, user-authored skills (created_by: learn), and hub packages are ignored unless explicitly adopted via hermes curator adopt &lt;skill&gt;.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Curator maintenance executes across two operational phases:</span></p><ol class="lst-kix_list_6-0 start" start="1" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Deterministic Transition Phase: A zero-token Python process that evaluates inactivity intervals. Skills uninvoked for 14 days (stale_after_days) transition to stale status. Skills uninvoked for 30 days (archive_after_days) are moved out of active discovery into ~/.hermes/skills/.archive/. Skills are never permanently deleted by the Curator and can be restored using hermes curator restore &lt;skill&gt;. Furthermore, skills pinned via hermes curator pin &lt;skill&gt; or referenced by active cron blueprints are exempt from archival.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Opt-in LLM Consolidation Phase: When curator.consolidate: true is enabled, the Curator forks an auxiliary agent to evaluate the agent-created catalog. Across 50 to 100 API transactions, this pass identifies narrow, overlapping procedures, merges them into unified umbrella skills, re-homes associated scripts and references, and patches procedural drift. Before performing in-place rewrites, the Curator archives a full snapshot (.tar.gz) of the skills directory, allowing instant rollbacks via hermes curator rollback.</span></li></ol><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Skill Capability Matrix</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The following matrix documents the operational capabilities accessible to Hermes through the skills mechanism. Each capability is classified into one of five definitive categories:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_7-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">NATIVE SKILL CAPABILITY: Directly provided by the Hermes Skills framework.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">TOOL-INHERITED CAPABILITY: Achieved because the skill can instruct an active Hermes tool.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">EXTERNAL-DEPENDENCY CAPABILITY: Operates only when external software, binaries, or MCP servers are present.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">UNCLEAR: Insufficient evidence in documentation or codebase.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">NOT FOUND: No credible evidence of support.</span></li></ul><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:66.9pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="p